    bio: 'Nicolas Malebranche was born in Paris on August 6, 1638, the youngest of thirteen children of Nicolas de Malebranche, a royal secretary, and Catherine de Lauzon. Afflicted with spinal curvature and fragile health throughout his childhood, he received an early education at home and later studied philosophy and theology at the Collège de la Marche and at the Sorbonne. In 1660, he entered the Congregation of the Oratory, a community of secular priests devoted to intellectual and spiritual formation, where he was ordained in 1664. According to his early biographers, his philosophical vocation was ignited in 1664 when he stumbled upon Descartes\'s Treatise on Man in a Paris bookshop — an encounter so electrifying that he reportedly had to stop reading repeatedly to recover his composure. He spent the next decade in intensive study, emerging in 1674–1675 with the first volumes of his magnum opus, De la recherche de la vérité (The Search After Truth).\n\nMalebranche\'s central philosophical achievement was the doctrine known as Occasionalism, combined with the theory of "Vision in God." He accepted Descartes\'s sharp dualism between mind and body but found it impossible to explain how two such radically different substances — unextended thinking and unthinking extension — could causally interact. His solution was elegant and radical: they do not interact at all. What we call causation between mind and body, or between any two finite things, is not genuine causation but merely regular succession. The true and only genuine cause of everything is God. When I will to raise my arm and my arm rises, my volition is the "occasion" for God to act, not the cause. Every apparent natural causation — billiard balls colliding, fire burning paper, neurons firing — is in reality a direct act of divine power, with the natural event serving only as the occasion that God has ordained to accompany his action. This made God not the distant clockmaker of Deism but the ever-present, immediate sustainer of every moment of reality.\n\nMalebranche spent his long life in the Oratory, rarely traveling but engaged in extensive and sometimes combative correspondence with the leading minds of his era. He engaged in famous public disputes with Antoine Arnauld, the great Jansenist theologian, over the nature of ideas and divine grace — a controversy that generated multiple volumes of intricate philosophical theology over more than a decade. Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz studied his work carefully and acknowledged its influence, even as he developed his own pre-established harmony as a rival solution to the mind-body problem. John Locke read and criticized Malebranche. George Berkeley acknowledged him as a crucial precursor to his own idealism. Despite poor health and the demands of a massive scholarly output — including works on ethics, natural philosophy, and Christian piety — Malebranche remained intellectually active into old age, revising and expanding his major works through multiple editions. He died in Paris on October 13, 1715.',
